WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.920
Two crossovers on one chromatid between the same pair of genes cancel out.

2
00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.220
How do you count what you cannot see?

3
00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.780
One crossover between two genes recombines them.

4
00:00:14.560 --> 00:00:19.340
A second crossover between the same two genes, on the same chromatid, puts them back

5
00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:23.560
together. The gamete has been rearranged twice, and it looks parental.

6
00:00:24.633 --> 00:00:27.653
The fix is a third gene, placed between the other two.

7
00:00:28.473 --> 00:00:29.473
Call it the middle marker.

8
00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:33.153
Now a double crossover swaps only that middle gene.

9
00:00:34.013 --> 00:00:35.813
The two outer genes still look parental.

10
00:00:36.773 --> 00:00:39.353
But the middle gene has switched, and the hidden event shows.

11
00:00:40.417 --> 00:00:45.317
A female carries three linked genes, P, Q and R, and is crossed to a tester.

12
00:00:46.117 --> 00:00:51.037
The tester adds only recessive alleles, so each offspring reads out one gamete from

13
00:00:51.037 --> 00:00:54.617
the mother. Sort 1,000 offspring into eight classes.

14
00:00:55.457 --> 00:00:59.657
The two biggest classes show no crossover, and the four in the middle are single

15
00:00:59.657 --> 00:01:05.557
crossovers. The two smallest classes are usually the double crossovers, 7 and 5.

16
00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:09.590
Match each rare class with the parental it most resembles.

17
00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:12.690
Each one differs at P and nowhere else.

18
00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:17.330
A double crossover swaps only the gene between the two crossovers.

19
00:01:18.230 --> 00:01:23.070
So P is the middle gene, and the order is Q, P, R. The order of the names are

20
00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:24.290
written in tells you nothing.

21
00:01:25.333 --> 00:01:29.553
A double crossover is a crossover in each interval, so it counts in both.

22
00:01:30.453 --> 00:01:36.573
From Q to P, 53 and 55 singles plus 12 doubles make 120 in 1,000.

23
00:01:37.513 --> 00:01:39.573
That is 12.0 centimorgans.

24
00:01:40.413 --> 00:01:46.733
From P to R, 96 and 92 singles plus the same 12 make 200, or 20.0.

25
00:01:46.733 --> 00:01:50.913
Together, Q to R is 32.0 centimorgans.

26
00:01:51.833 --> 00:01:55.193
Now score only Q and R, as a two-point cross would.

27
00:01:56.013 --> 00:01:59.253
The doubles carry the parental combinations, so they drop out.

28
00:02:00.153 --> 00:02:05.033
Only the 296 singles count, which gives 29.6 percent.

29
00:02:05.033 --> 00:02:09.893
That is 2.4 points short, the 12 doubles counted once in each interval.

30
00:02:10.813 --> 00:02:13.173
Count every double crossover in both intervals.
